---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { FORMALIZATION_LABELS, PUBLICATION_LABELS, VERIFICATION_LABELS } from '../lib/content/labels.ts';

// URL inglese /method/ dal 2026-11-01; /metodo/ reindirizza qui (vercel.json).
const statusGroups = [
  { name: 'Publication', labels: Object.values(PUBLICATION_LABELS) },
  { name: 'Formalization', labels: Object.values(FORMALIZATION_LABELS) },
  { name: 'Verification', labels: Object.values(VERIFICATION_LABELS) },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Method"
  description="How sources are chosen, what role AI plays, and how review and corrections work."
  current="metodo"
>
  <article class="article">
    <header class="article-header">
      <h1 class="article-title">Method</h1>
      <p class="article-summary">
        Briefly.tcs publishes one briefing a day on new results in theoretical computer science. Briefings are drafted
        by an AI model and published automatically.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Current status</h2>
      <p>
        Briefings are drafted by an AI model (ChatGPT) from the sources they cite and published automatically: no
        human editor and no independent expert checks them before they appear.
      </p>

      <h2>One briefing a day</h2>
      <p>
        The unit of publication is the daily briefing, with one or more results. Each result has its own scientific
        status and sources, and a direct link to its section. Standalone in-depth pieces may accompany the
        briefings when a subject calls for it. If a day brings no significant news, nothing is published to fill the gap.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Primary sources are preferred: manuscripts, arXiv, ECCC, conference proceedings, and authors’ pages. The version
        consulted is cited. A reachable link is not enough to verify a theorem: the content must be checked to support
        the stated claim.
      </p>

      <h2>Scientific status</h2>
      <p>
        The status of a result has three independent dimensions, shown as text next to the result. A preprint, for
        example, may already be formalized.
      </p>
      <dl class="status-vocabulary">
        {
          statusGroups.map((group) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{group.name}</dt>
              <dd>{group.labels.join(' · ')}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <h2>Role of AI</h2>
      <p>
        Each briefing is written by an AI model from the sources it cites. Before publication, software checks only the
        structure: required fields, https source links, well-formed formulas, one briefing per day. These checks do
        not verify the mathematics. Every result is therefore labelled “automated check only”, and the primary sources
        remain the authority.
      </p>

      <h2>What is not checked</h2>
      <p>
        No one reads a briefing before it is published, and an AI model can misstate a result or its status. If a claim
        matters to your work, read the cited paper. Pages are generated in advance and served as static HTML; no AI runs
        while you read the site.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Every correction records its date and a description, and updates the article’s date. Substantive corrections are
        also flagged at the top of the article; minor ones appear only in the final list. Corrections are currently
        made by hand when an error is reported.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
